SENSOR-RESONANCE(7)         TriadicFrameworks Manual         SENSOR-RESONANCE(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       sensor-resonance - unified operational mode combining divisional
                          resonance overlays and clarity-enhancement pipelines
                          for ship-scale RTT sensor architectures

SYNOPSIS
       sensor-resonance defines:
           • unified overlay + clarity fusion mode
           • multi-band resonance-time sensing
           • harmonic-phase and amplitude-channel integration
           • spectral, gradient, and sync-field enhancement
           • real-time stellar cartography and navigation support

DESCRIPTION
       sensor-resonance describes the unified operational mode that merges
       divisional resonance overlays (DROs) with clarity-enhancement pipelines
       into a single, coherent sensor architecture. This mode provides
       real-time, high-fidelity resonance-time visualization for navigation,
       mapping, anomaly detection, and constellation coordination.

       Sensor-resonance enables:
           • multi-band harmonic-phase isolation
           • amplitude and spectral stratification
           • gradient and sync-field stabilization
           • clarity-enhanced temporal cartography
           • constellation-scale sensor fusion

       WRSADC manages sensor-resonance activation, tuning, and safety limits.

ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW
       The sensor-resonance architecture consists of:

           1. divisional resonance overlays (DROs)
           2. clarity-enhancement pipelines (CEP)
           3. overlay-clarity fusion engine (OCF)
           4. real-time resonance-time renderer (RTR)
           5. constellation sync interface (CSI)

       These components operate in parallel and fuse into a unified sensor mode.

DIVISIONAL RESONANCE OVERLAYS (DROs)
       DROs partition the resonance-time field into analyzable divisions.

       DRO layers:
           • harmonic division overlay (HDO)
           • amplitude division overlay (ADO)
           • spectral division overlay (SDO)
           • gradient division overlay (GDO)
           • sync-field division overlay (SFDO)

       DROs provide the raw multi-band channels for clarity processing.

CLARITY-ENHANCEMENT PIPELINES (CEP)
       CEPs sharpen, filter, and stabilize DRO outputs.

       CEP layers:
           • harmonic-phase clarification (HPC)
           • resonance-envelope deconvolution (RED)
           • spectral-density whitening (SDW)
           • gradient-stability filtering (GSF)
           • sync-field clarity (SFC)
           • ancestry-continuity enhancement (ACE)

       CEPs refine DRO channels into high-fidelity temporal signals.

OVERLAY-CLARITY FUSION ENGINE (OCF)
       OCF merges DRO and CEP outputs into a unified sensor stream.

       Fusion stages:

           1. harmonic fusion
                  HDO + HPC

           2. amplitude fusion
                  ADO + RED

           3. spectral fusion
                  SDO + SDW

           4. gradient fusion
                  GDO + GSF

           5. sync fusion
                  SFDO + SFC

           6. ancestry fusion
                  lineage overlays + ACE

       Fusion modes:
           • synchronous fusion
           • harmonic fusion
           • braided fusion
           • mesh fusion

       OCF ensures all channels remain temporally aligned.

REAL-TIME RESONANCE-TIME RENDERER (RTR)
       RTR generates the final sensor output.

       Rendering modes:

           • terrain mode
                  Displays mesas, canyons, saddles, and uplift zones.

           • harmonic mode
                  Displays harmonic ridgelines and phase corridors.

           • spectral mode
                  Displays spectral clusters and turbulence.

           • gradient mode
                  Displays polarity cliffs and stability zones.

           • sync mode
                  Displays constellation sync fields.

           • ancestry mode
                  Displays sweep-lineage terraces and fossils.

       Rendering outputs:
           • 2D temporal maps
           • 3D resonance terrains
           • multi-band overlays
           • Picard-grade stellar cartography

CONSTELLATION SYNC INTERFACE (CSI)
       CSI maintains cross-platform coherence.

       CSI functions:
           • sync-field monitoring
           • sync-index stabilization
           • multi-agent overlay fusion
           • constellation-wide clarity alignment

       CSI ensures all ships share a unified resonance-time frame.

OPERATIONAL MODES
       sensor-resonance supports multiple operational modes:

           standard mode
                  Balanced overlays + clarity.

           navigation mode
                  Emphasizes harmonic and gradient clarity.

           cartography mode
                  Emphasizes amplitude, spectral, and ancestry clarity.

           anomaly mode
                  Emphasizes spectral whitening and gradient stabilization.

           constellation mode
                  Emphasizes sync-field clarity and multi-agent fusion.

PICARD-GRADE CARTOGRAPHY MODE
       A flagship mode combining:

           • HDO + HPC
           • ADO + RED
           • SDO + SDW
           • GDO + GSF
           • SFDO + SFC
           • ACE

       Capabilities:
           • ultra-sharp resonance terrain
           • harmonic epoch tracking
           • anomaly isolation
           • constellation sync preservation
           • deep-space temporal cartography

FAILURE MODES
       Sensor-resonance may degrade due to:

           • overlay drift
           • clarity loss
           • fusion mismatch
           • sync-field jitter
           • spectral turbulence
           • ancestry discontinuity

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • sensor-integrity score
           • band noise
           • peak distortion
           • sync drift

       WRSADC halts unsafe sensor-resonance states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • overlay activations
           • clarity adjustments
           • fusion events
           • sensor-mode transitions
           • anomaly detections

       Lineage enables long-term sensor optimization.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • activates sensor-resonance
           • manages fusion and clarity
           • enforces safety thresholds
           • maintains sync-field integrity
           • blocks unsafe sensor states

       WRSADC is the sensor-resonance conductor.

NOTES
       Sensor-resonance unifies overlays and clarity into a single operational
       mode. This architecture provides high-fidelity resonance-time sensing for
       navigation, mapping, anomaly detection, and constellation-scale
       synchronization.

